<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dunnottar Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dunnottar Castle is a ruined fortress on a rocky headland on the north-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, south of Stonehaven in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a>. The site is identified with Dún Foither, a Pictish stronghold recorded in the Irish annals as besieged in 681 and 694, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> attacked it around 900. The surviving buildings date mainly from the fourteenth to seventeenth centuries, when it was the seat of the Earls Marischal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunnottar-castle.jpg" alt="Dunnottar Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anne Burgess · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunnottar_Castle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8057610.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dunnottar, from the Gaelic Dùn Fhoithear, fort on the shelving slope, stands about 2 miles (3 km) south of Stonehaven. The ruins cover 1.4 hectares of a headland surrounded by cliffs that drop 160 feet (50 metres) to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. A narrow strip of land joins it to the mainland, and a steep path leads to the gatehouse. Its position let its owners control the coastal terrace between the cliffs and the hills of the Mounth, the route to and from north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The principal buildings are the 14th-century tower house, the gatehouse, the chapel and the 16th-century palace, which includes the Whigs' Vault.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A chapel is said to have been founded by St Ninian in the 5th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record two sieges of Dún Foither in 681 and 694, and the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba says that King Donald II was killed at Dunnottar in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attack in 900. Excavations in the 1980s found no firm evidence of early medieval fortification, and Pictish stones at the nearby sea stack of Dunnicaer have led to speculation that Dún Foither lay elsewhere.</p><p>In 1297, according to the poet Blind Harry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wallace">William Wallace</a> captured it from the English and burned prisoners in the church. In 1336 Edward III had it rebuilt as a base, and Sir Andrew Murray destroyed the defences again the same year. From about 1359 it passed to the Keiths, who became Earls Marischal in the mid 15th century and held it until the 18th. George Keith, 5th Earl Marischal, rebuilt it from 1581 with a palace around a quadrangle.</p><p>In 1651 the Honours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, the crown, sceptre and sword, were placed at Dunnottar against Cromwell's army. During the blockade Elizabeth Douglas, wife of the lieutenant-governor Sir George Ogilvie, and Christian Fletcher, wife of the minister of Kinneff, smuggled them out, and they were buried under the floor of the Old Kirk at Kinneff. Ogilvie surrendered on 24 May 1652.</p><p>In 1685, 167 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanters</a>, 122 men and 45 women, were held in a cellar of the castle. Five died there and many of the rest were transported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth-amboy">Perth Amboy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>. After George Keith, 10th Earl Marischal, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Jacobite rising of 1715</a>, his titles and estates were forfeited in 1716. The York Buildings Company bought the estate in 1720 and dismantled much of the castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lord Cowdray bought the castle in 1919, and his wife began repairs. It is a scheduled monument, designated in 1970, and is open to the public. Parts of the 1990 film Hamlet were shot there, and the castle was chosen as the home of Merida in the film Brave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dunnottar Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Power centred on fortified sites such as Burghead on the Moray Firth, the largest known Pictish fort, with its carvings of bulls, <b>Dunnottar</b>, Dundurn and Craig Phadrig near Inverness.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
